{"v":3,"topic":"sub:12|Differential calculus|Differentiation with trigonometric functions","q":{"300":{"stem":null,"options":[{"label":"A","latex":"$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\cos x^\\circ$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"generated","fig":null},{"label":"B","latex":"$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\\sin x^\\circ$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"generated","fig":null},{"label":"C","latex":"$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{\\pi}{180}\\cos\\dfrac{\\pi}{180}x$","is_correct":1,"key_source":"generated","fig":null},{"label":"D","latex":"$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{180}\\cos\\dfrac{\\pi}{180}x$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"generated","fig":null}],"opt_table":null,"layout":"text","figures":[],"parts":[{"id":466,"latex":"The derivative of $y=\\sin x^\\circ$ is","needs_review":1,"c17":{"code":"MA-C2.1","conf":0.7},"note":null,"can_use":[]}],"solutions":[{"id":438,"part_id":null,"source":"generated","latex":"Convert degrees to radians: $x^\\circ = \\dfrac{\\pi x}{180}$ radians, so $y = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{180}x\\right)$.\n\nBy the chain rule,\n$$\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{180}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{180}x\\right)$$","criteria":[[1,"Correct answer"]],"meta":{"confidence":0.95,"check":"passed"}}]},"875":{"stem":null,"options":[{"label":"A","latex":"$2x\\sec^2\\left(g\\left(x^2\\right)\\right)$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"B","latex":"$2xg'\\left(x^2\\right)\\sec^2\\left(g(2x)\\right)$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"C","latex":"$2xg\\left(x^2\\right)\\sec^2\\left(g\\left(x^2\\right)\\right)$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"D","latex":"$2xg'\\left(x^2\\right)\\sec^2\\left(g\\left(x^2\\right)\\right)$","is_correct":1,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null}],"opt_table":null,"layout":"text","figures":[],"parts":[{"id":1429,"latex":"If $f(x)=\\tan\\left(g\\left(x^2\\right)\\right)$, where $g(x)$ is a differentiable function, what is $f'(x)$?","needs_review":1,"c17":{"code":"MA-C2.1","conf":0.8},"note":null,"can_use":[]}],"solutions":[{"id":1215,"part_id":null,"source":"extracted","latex":"By the chain rule (twice):\n$$\\begin{aligned} f'(x)&=\\sec^2\\left(g(x^2)\\right)\\times\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(g(x^2)\\right)\\\\ &=\\sec^2\\left(g(x^2)\\right)\\times g'(x^2)\\times 2x\\\\ &=2x\\,g'(x^2)\\sec^2\\left(g(x^2)\\right) \\end{aligned}$$","criteria":[[1,"Correct answer"]],"meta":{"top_criterion_original":"D"}}]},"1242":{"stem":null,"options":[{"label":"A","latex":"$3\\sin(4x)$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"B","latex":"$-3\\sin(4x)$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"C","latex":"$-12\\sin(4x)$","is_correct":1,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"D","latex":"$12\\sin(4x)$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null}],"opt_table":null,"layout":"text","figures":[],"parts":[{"id":2097,"latex":"What is the derivative of $y=3\\cos(4x)$?","needs_review":0,"c17":{"code":"MA-C2.1","conf":0.85},"note":null,"can_use":[]}],"solutions":[{"id":1847,"part_id":null,"source":"extracted","latex":"Differentiating, using $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\cos ax=-a\\sin ax$:\n$$\\begin{aligned}y&=3\\cos(4x)\\\\\\dfrac{dy}{dx}&=-12\\sin(4x)\\end{aligned}$$","criteria":[[1,"Correct answer"]],"meta":{}}]},"1309":{"stem":null,"options":[{"label":"A","latex":"$-\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"B","latex":"$-\\sin(\\ln x)$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"C","latex":"$-\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\right)$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"D","latex":"$-\\dfrac{\\sin(\\ln x)}{x}$","is_correct":1,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null}],"opt_table":null,"layout":"text","figures":[],"parts":[{"id":2216,"latex":"What is the derivative of $\\cos(\\ln x)$, where $x>0$?","needs_review":0,"c17":{"code":"MA-C2.2","conf":0.85},"note":null,"can_use":[]}],"solutions":[{"id":1966,"part_id":null,"source":"extracted","latex":"By the chain rule:\n$$\\begin{aligned}y&=\\cos(\\ln x)\\\\ \\dfrac{dy}{dx}&=-\\sin(\\ln x)\\times\\dfrac{d}{dx}(\\ln x)\\\\ &=-\\sin(\\ln x)\\times\\dfrac{1}{x}\\\\ &=-\\dfrac{\\sin(\\ln x)}{x}\\end{aligned}$$","criteria":[[1,"Correct answer"]],"meta":{"top_criterion_original":"D"}}]},"1535":{"stem":null,"options":[{"label":"A","latex":"$\\sec^2\\left(\\ln\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"B","latex":"$\\dfrac{1}{\\sec^2(\\ln x)}$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"C","latex":"$\\dfrac{\\sec^2(\\ln x)}{x}$","is_correct":1,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"D","latex":"$\\dfrac{\\sec^2\\frac{1}{x}}{x}$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null}],"opt_table":null,"layout":"text","figures":[],"parts":[{"id":2515,"latex":"What is the derivative of $\\tan(\\ln x)$, where $x>0$?","needs_review":0,"c17":{"code":"MA-C2.2","conf":0.9},"note":null,"can_use":[]}],"solutions":[{"id":2264,"part_id":null,"source":"extracted","latex":"Using the chain rule with $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\ln x=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\tan u=\\sec^2 u$:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{d}{dx}\\tan(\\ln x) &= \\dfrac{1}{x}\\sec^2(\\ln x) \\\\ &= \\dfrac{\\sec^2(\\ln x)}{x} \\end{aligned}$$","criteria":[[1,"Correct answer"]],"meta":{"top_criterion_original":"C"}}]},"2485":{"stem":null,"options":[{"label":"A","latex":"$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"B","latex":"$-\\dfrac{1}{2}$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"C","latex":"$\\dfrac{1}{2}$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"D","latex":"$-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$","is_correct":1,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null}],"opt_table":null,"layout":"text","figures":[],"parts":[{"id":4049,"latex":"If $f(x) = \\cos x$, the value of $f'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$ is,","needs_review":0,"c17":null,"note":null,"can_use":[]}],"solutions":[{"id":3443,"part_id":null,"source":"extracted","latex":"The derivative of $\\cos x$ is $-\\sin x$.\n$$f'(x) = -\\sin x$$\nThen\n$$\\begin{aligned} f'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) &= -\\sin\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\\\ &= -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{aligned}$$\nThis matches option D.","criteria":[[1,"Correct answer"]],"meta":{}}]},"2488":{"stem":null,"options":[{"label":"A","latex":"$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{3}\\right)$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"B","latex":"$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\sec^2\\left(\\dfrac{x}{3}\\right)$","is_correct":1,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"C","latex":"$3\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{3}\\right)$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"D","latex":"$3\\sec^2\\left(\\dfrac{x}{3}\\right)$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null}],"opt_table":null,"layout":"text","figures":[],"parts":[{"id":4055,"latex":"What is the derivative of $y = \\tan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{3}\\right)$ ?","needs_review":0,"c17":null,"note":null,"can_use":[]}],"solutions":[{"id":3449,"part_id":null,"source":"extracted","latex":"Using $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\tan(ax) = a\\sec^2(ax)$ with $a=\\dfrac{1}{3}$:\n$$\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\sec^2\\left(\\dfrac{x}{3}\\right)$$\nThis matches option B.","criteria":[[1,"Correct answer"]],"meta":{}}]},"2702":{"stem":null,"options":[],"opt_table":null,"layout":null,"figures":[],"parts":[{"id":4397,"latex":"Show that the function $f(x) = \\sin 2x - 3x$ is decreasing throughout its domain.","needs_review":0,"c17":null,"note":null,"can_use":["Maxima and minima from the range"]}],"solutions":[{"id":3789,"part_id":null,"source":"extracted","latex":"Differentiate:\n$$f'(x)=2\\cos 2x-3$$\nSince $-1\\le\\cos 2x\\le 1$:\n$$\\begin{aligned} -2&\\le 2\\cos 2x\\le 2 &&(\\text{multiply by } 2)\\\\ -5&\\le 2\\cos 2x-3\\le -1 &&(\\text{subtract } 3)\\end{aligned}$$\nSo $f'(x)<0$ for all $x$.\n\n$\\therefore$ $f(x)$ is decreasing throughout its domain.","criteria":[[2,"Correct answer"],[1,"Finds $f'(x)$, or equivalent merit"]],"meta":{"format_note":"Rubric written by us."}}]},"2741":{"stem":null,"options":[{"label":"A","latex":"$\\sin(e^x)$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"B","latex":"$-\\sin(e^x)$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"C","latex":"$e^x\\sin(e^x)$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"D","latex":"$-e^x\\sin(e^x)$","is_correct":1,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null}],"opt_table":null,"layout":"text","figures":[],"parts":[{"id":4456,"latex":"Given $f(x) = \\cos(e^x)$, what of the following is the correct expression for $f'(x)$ ?","needs_review":0,"c17":null,"note":null,"can_use":[]}],"solutions":[{"id":3848,"part_id":null,"source":"extracted","latex":"Using the chain rule with inner function $e^x$:\n$$\\begin{aligned} f'(x) &= -\\sin(e^x)\\times e^x \\\\ &= -e^x\\sin(e^x) \\end{aligned}$$\nThis matches option D.","criteria":[[1,"Correct answer"]],"meta":{}}]},"2742":{"stem":null,"options":[{"label":"A","latex":"$1$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"B","latex":"$\\tan x$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"C","latex":"$\\dfrac{1}{\\cos^2 x}$","is_correct":1,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"D","latex":"$\\cos^2 x$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null}],"opt_table":null,"layout":"text","figures":[],"parts":[{"id":4457,"latex":"Which of the following is the derivative of $\\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$ with respect to $x$?","needs_review":0,"c17":null,"note":null,"can_use":["Quotient rule"]}],"solutions":[{"id":3849,"part_id":null,"source":"extracted","latex":"Using the quotient rule, $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(\\dfrac{u}{v}\\right)=\\dfrac{u'v-uv'}{v^2}$, with\n$$\\begin{aligned} u&=\\sin x & v&=\\cos x\\\\ u'&=\\cos x & v'&=-\\sin x\\end{aligned}$$\n$$\\begin{aligned} y' &= \\dfrac{\\cos x\\cos x - (-\\sin x)\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x} \\\\ &= \\dfrac{\\cos^2 x + \\sin^2 x}{\\cos^2 x} \\\\ &= \\dfrac{1}{\\cos^2 x} \\end{aligned}$$\nThis matches option C.","criteria":[[1,"Correct answer"]],"meta":{}}]},"2908":{"stem":null,"options":[{"label":"A","latex":"$\\dfrac{\\sin(x)}{\\cos^2(x)}$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"B","latex":"$\\sec(x)$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"C","latex":"$\\dfrac{-\\cos(x)}{\\sin^2(x)}$","is_correct":1,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"D","latex":"$-\\sec(x)$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null}],"opt_table":null,"layout":"text","figures":[],"parts":[{"id":4730,"latex":"What is the derivative of $\\operatorname{cosec}(x)$?","needs_review":0,"c17":null,"note":null,"can_use":["Chain rule","Quotient rule"]}],"solutions":[{"id":4120,"part_id":null,"source":"extracted","latex":"Write $\\operatorname{cosec} x=(\\sin x)^{-1}$ and apply the chain rule.\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{dy}{dx} &= -(\\sin x)^{-2}\\times\\cos x \\\\ &= \\dfrac{-\\cos x}{\\sin^2 x} \\end{aligned}$$\nThis is option C.","criteria":[[1,"Correct answer"]],"meta":{}}]},"3131":{"stem":null,"options":[{"label":"A","latex":"$-1$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"B","latex":"$0$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"C","latex":"$3$","is_correct":1,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"D","latex":"$-9$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null}],"opt_table":null,"layout":"text","figures":[],"parts":[{"id":5090,"latex":"What is the value of the derivative of $y = 2\\sin 3x - 3\\tan x$ at $x = 0$?","needs_review":0,"c17":null,"note":null,"can_use":[]}],"solutions":[{"id":4473,"part_id":null,"source":"extracted","latex":"Differentiating each term,\n$$\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 2(3)\\cos 3x - 3\\sec^2 x$$\nAt $x = 0$:\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{dy}{dx} &= 6\\cos 0 - 3\\sec^2 0 \\\\ &= 6 - 3 \\\\ &= 3 \\end{aligned}$$\nThis is option C.","criteria":[[1,"Correct answer"]],"meta":{}}]},"3204":{"stem":null,"options":[{"label":"A","latex":"$-1$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"B","latex":"$0$","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"C","latex":"$1$","is_correct":1,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null},{"label":"D","latex":"Undefined","is_correct":0,"key_source":"extracted","fig":null}],"opt_table":null,"layout":"text","figures":[],"parts":[{"id":5214,"latex":"What is $\\displaystyle\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}+h\\right)-\\cos\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)}{h}$?","needs_review":0,"c17":null,"note":null,"can_use":[]}],"solutions":[{"id":4585,"part_id":null,"source":"extracted","latex":"The limit is the definition of the derivative of $f(x)=\\cos x$ at $x=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$.\n$$f'(x)=-\\sin x$$\n$$\\begin{aligned} f'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) &= -\\sin\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\\\ &= 1 \\end{aligned}$$\nThis is option C.","criteria":[[1,"Correct answer"]],"meta":{}}]}}}